"Protecting your mortgage might be the last thing you think about when faced with an onslaught of wildfires, like the ones threatening thousands of properties in Southern California.\nThis year has been the costliest year in recorded history from wildfires nationwide, according to the Department of Agriculture. And that does not include the recent wildfires in California, which have threatened nearly 25,000 homes and destroyed more than 1,000 structures, according to reports.\nWhen faced with a disaster like a wildfire, you may be eligible to get relief from your mortgage payments. Call your lender as soon as possible to ask if it\u2019s offering financial relief.\nEach bank or mortgage lender will offer different types of financial relief depending on your situation, but you won\u2019t know until you call.\nMany lenders are not requiring documentation, like a mortgage statement, since often times people have evacuated their homes where their paperwork is stored. In some cases, the home does not have to be damaged to get financial relief. You may be eligible for financial help because you can\u2019t get back to work due to an evacuation, or because you need time to get connected with an insurance company, says Tom Kelly, spokesman at JPMorgan Chase.\nMajor lenders including Chase, Bank of America and Wells Fargo confirmed they are offering some form of financial relief for people impacted by the wildfires depending on each person\u2019s situation.\nThe type of financial assistance will vary with each lender. But during past wildfires this year, some California banks waived late payment fees, deferred payments for up to 90 days, or delayed reporting a credit issue to the bureaus, says Beth Mills, a spokeswoman for the California Bankers Association.\nWells Fargo, for example, is offering to halt any late fees, collection calls or negative reporting to the credit bureaus for customers impacted by the wildfires, says Tom Goyda, a spokesman at Wells Fargo. The bank is also offering certain foreclosure protections.\n\u201cWe are offering disaster assistance to customers impacted by the wildfires when they contact us,\u201d he says."
"Spring Forward has reached thousands of kids + adults through its many years of programming and service.\nhas grown to serve more than 600 children in our after-school programs and summer enrichment camps and another 50 adult English Learners in our Family Literacy program. We aim to support a community in which all people are embraced and have an equal opportunity for success.\nWe know that reading is a key to success for people in school and beyond, that's why we make it a priority in all of our educational programming. Through our leveled literacy intervention curriculum, regular attenders of our programs reliably see measurable results.\nThrough our partnerships with wonderful community organizations such as the Figge Art Museum, Putnam Museum, Living Lands and Waters, Rock Island Library, Rock Island Parks and Recreation, and the Niabi Zoo we are able to take Spring Forward Learners out into the community to experience new learning opportunities.\nAlong with over 30 Program Aides and Camp Counselors, our highly trained and qualified staff works hard to ensure that our learners are receiving exceptional educational enrichment throughout all of our programming.\nin a school district where roughly 10% of our community members are newcomers to the united states, we are proud to offer educational opportunities to adults and children whose native languages include arabic, spanish, karen, swahili, kirundi, burmese, and more.\nPay to play? No way.\nwe want to see all kids have the opportunity to participate in league sports and through our partnership with the Rock Island Futbol Club we've been able to help some do just that. in our first year offering spring forward soccer club, 45 young athletes were able to learn about the important lessons of teamwork, dedication and sportsmanship, and have fun doing it.\nKnowing that having books in the home is a key indicator of a child's academic success, Spring Forward provides books for all of our program participants."
"This term, we will be working with a new class book - you will find out all about the story as we read it section by section over the term! We will be doing lots of predicting of what will happen next, as well as practising using the four sentence types in our writing: statements, commands, questions and exclamations.\nWe will start the term by revising our methods for the four operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication and division) before using these skills to begin to solve more complication missing number problems and word problems. We will then move onto solving problems involving money, before recapping reading and showing the time on clock faces.\nOur topic this term is \u2018Sensational Safaris!\u2019 We will be focussing on Kenya and comparing the similarities/differences to our location in England. We will learn about the animals found in Kenya, and those that could be spotted on safari, as well as learning about the Maasai culture. Children will also have the opportunity to compare their lives to those lives of children in Kenya.\nThis term, we will be learning about Islam, focusing on the learning question \u2018Who is a Muslim and what do they believe?\u2019. During this topic, we will be learning about the Prophet Muhammad and some of the stories about his life, as well as finding out about mosques and reflecting on places/objects that we find special.\nWe will be learning all about plants this term. We will be planting our own bulbs and seeds, making predictions on which conditions are best for plants to grow in and making observational conclusions. We will also explore the life cycle of plants and compare different plants, fruits and vegetables.\nThis term, we will be having P.E on Monday afternoons, however please ensure that P.E kits are in school at all times."
