"Not reaching a particular Website with Xfinity, why?\nI've been taking classes on line for about 10 months. Suddenly I cannot reach the website on Xfinity.\nI contacted the school they assured me nothing has changed on their end. they made some suggestions like changing my browser, emptying cache removing cookies. Nothing has worked. I can reach the site on my phone or using my computer with a hot spot. The website is fine I just can't reach it on Xfinity from home. I can reach my bank and other sites on Xfinity just not this site. I tried other devices connected to Xfinity in my home and I still can't get there.\nAny relevant suggestions are welcomed.\nRe: Not reaching a particular Website with Xfinity, why?\nI can reach the site. When one customer cannot reach a site but others can, it usually means the site is blocking that customer's public Comcast IP. You might try asking the site's admins for help with this. You can look up your IP at https://www.google.com/search?q=IP.\nThank you so much for your reply to my question. Today I when to the computer typed in the address and there it was! Nothing I did, it just suddenly worked."
"Well, it looks like the Golden State Warriors will just have to make due with four All-Stars on their team and not potentially five.\nPortland Trail Blazers&apos; All-Star point guard Damian Lillard was asked on Twitter on Wednesday if he would consider playing for his hometown Warriors someday. The Oakland native a pretty definitive answer.\nThe Warriors already have Kevin Durant, Stephen Curry, Klay Thompson and Draymond Green, forming a big four the likes of which the NBA arguably has never seen. Lillard, 25, signed a five-year, $120 million extension last summer, so he wouldn&apos;t be hitting the free-agent market for some time.\nBut maybe Durant&apos;s move to the Bay Area has created a backlash among NBA stars, pushing them to compete against each other instead of join forces. We&apos;ll have to wait until next summer to see where the next crop of NBA superstars lands."
