"Today I have a work of historical fiction that tackles slavery. I can\u2019t lie. This is usually the type of book I shy away from because it gets me way too emotional. But I love Christopher Paul Curtis and it is a National Book finalist so I took one for the team and dug in. Gosh, was I rewarded with a fabulous read.\nDon\u2019t forget to follow One Great Book on Facebook, Twitter, Pinterest, Instagram or by email so that you don\u2019t miss a single story that will continue to open your eyes and your heart to a very ugly time in our history.\nCharlie\u2019s family are sharecroppers in South Carolina. But when Charlie\u2019s father dies, Charlie must accompany Cap\u2019n Buck, a man to whom his father owed money, on a mission. The mission it turns out is to track down slaves who have made their way to Detroit and freedom. But Charlie starts to realize that what he is doing doesn\u2019t feel right. The only problem is that Cap\u2019n Buck is not a man you double cross.\nThis is not the type of story that usually appeals to me. Whenever a character is suffering, it just makes my heart ache. But Curtis is a masterful storyteller and I was drawn into this one immediately. There is absolute cruelty here, but it is presented in a way that readers can handle. Slavery is not an easy topic.\nThe dialect is quite thick in this book. Strangely I didn\u2019t have an issue with it but some readers might. It certainly brings home the education level and ignorance of Charlie so I can see why Curlis used it. Stick with it. After about a chapter or two readers will catch on.\nCharlie is a hero in the end and I can\u2019t say the reader won\u2019t see it coming. But knowing his heart is kind is part of what makes this story so strong and readable in the end. This is the first novel I have read by Curtis where the main character is white, and it is an eye-opening perspective.\nGreat for kids who enjoy historical fiction. If you liked the author\u2019s other books this one will certainly please. Good choice for kids who like The Parker Inheritance or The Book of Boy.\nThis one could use a better cover.\nClick on the following link to connect to Amazon: The Journey of Little Charlie. A portion of each purchase will go to support this blog at no cost to you. Thank you for your support."
